import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"

pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Take one value from the front of a queue. An open, empty queue waits for a value; a closed queue drains buffered values, then fails with QueueClosed.",
  taskTitle: "Receive queued work in arrival order",
  purpose: "A Queue passes values between computations in first-in, first-out order. This example offers 10 and 20, closes the queue, and takes three times. Closing preserves the buffered values, so the first two takes succeed and the third reports closure.",
  setup: "import * as queues and import * as effects give the modules local names. unbounded<Int> () creates a queue of integers without a capacity limit; () is Unit, meaning no additional input. take receives only the queue, not a count.",
  codeTitle: "Complete program: drain a closed queue",
  outputTitle: "Output",
  after: [
    "takeText builds an Effect. The function \\value: Int -> show value converts one received integer to text; <$> applies it to a successful result. |> passes the Effect on its left to recover on its right. recover handles QueueClosed by producing the successful text \"closed\".",
    "In main, do sequences the effects. queue <- runs creation and names its result; first <- takeText queue runs one take and names the returned text. The offers and println calls also run in this sequence. AppError is a named choice between QueueFailure and ConsoleFailure; mapError wraps each operation’s typed failure in the matching choice. deriving Show supplies a printable form for that error.",
    "Effect<{}, Never, String> means takeText needs no service, has no remaining typed failure, and returns text. Never describes the handled failure channel; it does not rule out cancellation or runtime defects. Console output is still fallible in main."
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Consuming an asynchronous queue in TypeScript",
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript has no built-in asynchronous Queue type. With an application or library interface that supplies take(): Promise<T>, the same consumer can await one value and handle its closed-queue error. This is a consumer function, not a queue implementation.",
  comparisonAfter: "The TypeScript function starts when called. The Seseragi function builds an Effect for later execution. Both consumers preserve unexpected failures rather than treating every failure as a closed queue; the error representation and cancellation contract belong to the queue implementation.",
  detailTitle: "Empty, closed, and canceled takes",
  details: [
    "take on an open, empty queue waits for an offered value. Closing allows buffered values to drain in FIFO order; once none remain, take fails with QueueClosed. tryTake is the non-waiting alternative and distinguishes a value, an open empty queue, and closure.",
    "Canceling a waiting take does not consume a later value. If accepting a value and canceling occur in the same scheduler turn, their registration order decides which event wins. Closing and canceling are separate operations; cancellation is not QueueClosed."
  ]
}
